d  some amazing architecture like at the  avril vineyards which carry on  century-old traditions in modern  facilities  [Music]  thus must my special about wine from the  Kaiser store can you tell it's from here  by the smell of the Kaiser school easy  on the Kaiser stool is an inactive  volcano that's why we have very  interesting soil formations we have  plenty of this but underneath that  there's very dark decomposed volcanic  soil it's really crucial for us our best  wines grow well in that dark soil and we  think you can taste it in our wine the  minerality which is typical of Kaiser  stew a burgundy you'll be hard-pressed  to find a wine like that anywhere else  in Germany sounds great I'm picking up  honey yes a trace of honey exactly  Lesley what else does the expert smell  how's it for me well I can detect a note  of honeydew melon it does have a rather  fruity taste there are some slight  traces of vanilla from the wooden barrel  and when you take a sip you get it  tastes completely different than it  smells exactly the flavor of local fruit  slowly reveals itself the taste of ripe  apples ripe pears combined with a really  nice minerality and that flavor stays in  your mouth for a very long time it was  you must try this it's delicious  [Music]  by now I've crossed the border from  Germany to France I'm in Strasbourg  Alsace is regional capital has a long  history the Celts were here as well as  the Romans the Huns and the Franconian  zhh in the Middle Ages Strasburg was  part of the Holy Roman Empire of German  nations and Strasberg's Cathedral does  bear a striking resemblance to the one  in Freiburg no wonder they were built  around the same time where are you from  Atlanta Georgia and the United States  beautiful this is completely different  what do you what do you like about it I  love the age the history is so charming  beautiful what's your impression of the  city it's the first time I come here I  feel amazingly peace of church we love  Europe we